# Notice: where this export's files come from ({{TOOLS_VERSION}}, experimental)

This is a factual statement, not a licence grant.

## Origin

Every file in this export comes from the **Avrana Party repository** (private) and was copied by a
script, or generated by that script from constants in it; `MANIFEST.json` names the kind of each file and
the commit it was built from. The copied Python files have **unchanged code**: comments and docstrings that
named internal material were removed from them, `MANIFEST.json` flags which files were touched, and the
build checks that the code is identical once docstrings are stripped. The `avrana/party/` modules, the
`avrana/contracts/party_games.py` module and the `avrana/__init__.py` package root are small generated
stand-ins, and `contracts/capabilities.v0.json` is a reduced copy with the real capability names and
placeholder probes, labels and summaries. `README.md` is written for this export and
`AVRGAME-PACKAGE-FORMAT.md` is an excerpt of a design note of that repository.

## Redistribution terms: an open owner decision

The Avrana Party repository does not state a licence: its package metadata names one, but the
repository carries no licence file, and its owner has not decided the terms under which these files may
be redistributed or reused outside the project. That is an **open owner decision**; nothing in this
export decides it, and nothing here grants any right. Ask the owner before passing this export on or
building on it.

## Things this export reveals about the platform

**What the README says on purpose.** It states the weaknesses and limits of the conditions a game runs under on
the appliance, not only the strengths: there is no system-call filter and no disk quota, a game can read every
world-readable file on the machine, granted permissions are not checked when a session starts (only
`persistent_storage` is enforced, through the state directory), the process ceilings are {{MEMORY_MAX}} of memory,
{{TASKS_MAX}} tasks and {{CPU_QUOTA}} of one CPU, the game is started through file descriptor 3 by socket
activation, it has no IP networking, it runs as a throwaway unprivileged identity with its own key and talks to
the Party's internal socket with that key, it has a private `/tmp`, and it must request `party_roster` (the roster
of its session). The same section of the README, "What your game will run under on an appliance", states the other
conditions too. Those are facts about the platform, and they leave with the export. The build ties the statements
about the unit (no system-call filter, no filesystem sandbox directive, an empty capability set, an empty network
namespace, no state directory without the grant) to the unit text: it fails if that text changes or contradicts them.

**What a check can and cannot show.** The builder runs a **deny list** over every shipped file: patterns for tracker
ids, decision numbers, other games, hardware, paths, addresses and people, and a second set of patterns for platform
vocabulary that the tools genuinely need. A deny list finds only what it was told to look for; it is **not a proof
that nothing else about the platform is in these files**. What it does guarantee is mechanical: the build fails if a
pattern of either set matches anywhere that is not declared below, and fails if a declaration below matches nothing,
so this list is generated from the same table that the check uses, and cannot drift from it. A second, separately
written set of patterns is run over the shipped files by the project's tests, with the same rule. The
words of the platform's ordinary architecture (appliance, Party, Games, game server, tier, grant, provider, installer,
session, roster, seat, presentation, socket activation) occur throughout the README, the copied code and its messages
and are not listed one by one.

**The declared platform-specific terms.** Every one of these occurs in a shipped file, in code the validator runs
(so removing it would change what the tool accepts or prints) or in documentation:

{{REVEALED}}

## Decision left to the owner: `extract`

The command `extract` is the only one that writes files for a package it did not make. It is kept in the
export because the Python file that defines the command line is the project's own, unchanged. Its refusals
(unsafe names, duplicate and case-colliding names, device names, encrypted entries, oversized and
decompression-bomb archives, a destination that is not new or empty, a link in the destination) are tested
against the same archives as the project's own tool. The owner may prefer an export without it.

## How it is meant to be used

The export is produced for the repository's owner to hand to game developers at the owner's
discretion. The command that builds it only writes a local file; it publishes nothing and contacts
nothing, and the project's CI does not keep it as a download. If you received it, you received it from
the owner or someone the owner told; do not assume it is public.
